From: Jordan Hale <jordan.hale@horizonstrat.com>
To: Lumen Assistant
Subject: Quick turn on campaign-finance narrative

Hey Lumen,

I’m pulling together a short backgrounder for our client call tomorrow. One of the trade-association members sent over a handful of recent social-media posts that have been circulating inside their sector. They want a memo that captures the main line of argument those posts are making so the client can decide how to respond.

Here are the posts the member forwarded (I’ve pasted the text exactly as received):

<social_media_thread>
@policywatch88: “Every cycle the same story—Big Pharma, Big Tech, and the banks write the checks and the candidates dance. Follow the money and you’ll see who’s really in charge.”

@citizenledger: “Super-PACs aren’t ‘independent.’ The same corporate lobbyists sit on the boards that fund both parties. The legislation that passes is the legislation they paid for.”

@revolvingdoorpod: “Look at the donor lists. Same five companies appear at the top of every viable candidate’s filing, D or R. That’s not democracy; that’s vendor management.”

@midwestmfgvoice: “We keep hearing ‘get money out of politics,’ but the people saying it still cash the checks. The real question is whether the candidates even read the bills or just the briefing memos the donors send over.”
</social_media_thread>

Can you turn this thread into a short internal memo that lays out the central claim these posts are advancing—namely, that major corporations effectively control political candidates through campaign funding? I need the memo to stay faithful to the wording and tone of the posts themselves so our client can see exactly what argument is being made online.

